{"custom_id": "06bb34bb4d5b06f5866fedbc74b2008fc546dea6e28a5c83", "meta": {"loop": "T-r01-type-2786201", "task": "annotate_with_guidelines", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "4462681df3d848966c6936a11156a2c684e5f4d964a92435c1fa3330e35a342f", "prompt_chars": 10278}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_017fwLpZ2N5PTU9aMFy9gJ29", "saved_utc": "2026-11-13T22:26:43+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011Ch1i9jjpJbSrVaWV1P3ir", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "I'm working through ten candidate annotations, starting with \"human,\" which is just an organism name and gets no annotation. The next one, \"von Willebrand disease\" in the title, looks like a specific disease, so I'm leaning toward annotating it under the guideline for what to annotate.\n\nI'm settling on \"von Willebrand disease\" as a SpecificDisease, since the text treats it as one defined disease rather than a family of types. Its abbreviation \"vWD\" gets annotated separately as another SpecificDisease mention. Next I'm looking at \"inherited bleeding disorder,\" which reads as a broader disease class rather than a single disease.\n\nI'm weighing the remaining candidates for the annotation decision, now at the \"vWD\" mention in the molecular studies sentence, which I'm treating as a SpecificDisease since it's the disease itself rather than a modifier. Next up is \"type IIA vWD,\" which I'll look at once I get to it.\n\nI'm settling the last few annotation decisions: \"type IIA vWD\" in \"type IIA vWD patients\" and in \"type IIA vWD plasma\" both modify a noun, so they get Modifier rather than SpecificDisease, like \"colorectal cancer families.\" \"Abnormality\" is a general term and gets no annotation.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "{\"decisions\":[{\"id\":1,\"entity\":\"NONE\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"human\\\" names the species in the title phrase 'human von Willebrand disease' and is an organism name, which is excluded unless the disease caused by the organism is being discussed.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate 1: Do not annotate organism names\"},{\"id\":2,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"von Willebrand disease\\\" in the title is a single named disease with no further categorization in this span; it is the object of 'basis of' and is not used as a modifier.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease mentions\"},{\"id\":3,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"von Willebrand disease\\\" in the abstract's opening sentence refers to one defined disease, and the full name is the minimum necessary span for it.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease mentions; What to Annotate 5: minimum necessary span\"},{\"id\":4,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"vWD\\\" is the abbreviation of von Willebrand disease and is annotated as a separate synonymous mention from the long form.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 6: Synonymous mentions (abbreviations annotated separately)\"},{\"id\":5,\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"inherited bleeding disorder\\\" in the appositive describes a family of multiple specific bleeding diseases, so it is a class rather than one specific disease.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Disease Class mentions\"},{\"id\":6,\"entity\":\"NONE\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"humans\\\" is an organism name referring to the species and is not a disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate 1: Do not annotate organism names\"},{\"id\":7,\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"vWD\\\" in 'Molecular studies of vWD' is the abbreviation for von Willebrand disease used as the object of 'studies of', so it is a synonymous specific disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 1: Specific Disease mentions; What to Annotate 6: Synonymous mentions\"},{\"id\":8,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"type IIA vWD\\\" is a disease name used to modify the noun 'patients', describing the patient group, analogous to 'colorectal cancer families' being annotated as a Modifier.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Disease mentions used as Modifiers for other concepts\"},{\"id\":9,\"entity\":\"NONE\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"abnormality\\\" is a very general term describing a structural change and does not name a specific disease or disease class.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate 4: Do not annotate general terms (e.g., abnormalities)\"},{\"id\":10,\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"type IIA vWD\\\" modifies the noun 'plasma' in 'type IIA vWD plasma', functioning as a disease name used to modify another concept rather than as a standalone disease mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate 3: Disease mentions used as Modifiers for other concepts\"}]}", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4384, "output_tokens": 3611, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 2519}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}